# One row per filed elimination, saying what a WIDER elimination would do to the composed figure it
# comes off, and whether the filing itself licenses that reading. The reading
# eliminations/paired.sqlc chose decides the first; the claim's own `boundOrigin` decides the second.
#
# Subtracting bound by bound is anti-isotone in the elimination
#   Take the sum bound by bound and the low rises as the elimination's low rises, while the high
#   falls as its high falls. Widen the elimination at both ends and the composed figure NARROWS. A
#   composer who is less sure how much was double counted therefore files a more precise figure,
#   which is the one place in this model where less knowledge produces a tighter answer.
#
# The crossed reading is isotone
#   Pairing the sum's low against the elimination's high sends the width the other way, so widening
#   the elimination widens the composed figure, which is what every other term here does. The
#   crossed pairing is not chosen for that reason: eliminations/paired.sqlc reaches it only where
#   bound by bound would leave the claim disordered. The monotonicity is a consequence of that
#   repair rather than its motive.
#
# What licenses the bound-by-bound reading, and it is a filed answer
#   Reading an elimination bound by bound assumes its bounds move with the bounds of the figure it is
#   taken out of. A filing can say exactly that, at its claim's `boundOrigin`: a derivation naming
#   `sharedParts` means the figure is a copy of one stated in the part it removes, so its bounds ARE
#   that part's bounds and the assumption is the filer's own. An absent origin says nothing sets this
#   bound, which neither supports the assumption nor contradicts it. So `licensed` is not a verdict on
#   a filing; it is which of the two the filer wrote down.
#
# A point elimination has no reading to make
#   Where the elimination is a single value the two pairings are the same arithmetic, so there is
#   nothing for this relation to distinguish and `isotone` is NULL rather than either answer. That
#   is most of them, and reading the population without this column would report a split that the
#   corpus does not actually make.
#
# Why this reports and never accuses
#   Neither reading is a defect and neither licence state is. Bound by bound is right wherever the
#   elimination's bounds are the sum's own bounds, which is what a double count taken out of a figure
#   usually is, and the filings that come out unlicensed are correct under the reading they state. The
#   anti-isotone consequence is worth counting because nothing else here can see it, and worth
#   counting rather than judging.
#
# The claim's own figures come along, and they are the proof the key is right
#   `claim_seq` is an ordinal, so a join on it returns rows whether or not it points at the right
#   claim: off by one it still returns one claim per elimination, which is why a cardinality check
#   cannot settle it. The figures are what settle it, so they are carried here and
#   queries/observations/20-elimination-monotonicity.sqlc requires them to agree on every row with a
#   figure. A key checked only by how many rows it returns is not checked.
#
# The three origin columns are the evidence for the two derived from them
#   `licensed` and `what_the_bound_rests_on` are both read off `origin`, `origin_absent` and
#   `origin_derivation`, so those three ride beside them. Nothing in the tree reads them directly and
#   they stay because this relation runs alone: a verdict whose inputs are not in the row can only be
#   trusted, and the point of the licence column is that it is checkable against the filing.
#
# A typed absence stays a row
#   An elimination filed with no figure keeps its row, with the width, the reading and the licence
#   NULL. The suspension grounds decide what it lifts; this relation only says what could be read.
-- eliminations/paired.sqlc against epistemics/claims.sqlc at the claim the eliminated quantity is.
SELECT e.composition, e.composed_layer, e.quantity,
       e.low, e.mode, e.high, e.unit, e.absent, e.reason,
       e.high - e.low AS width,
       e.crossed,
       e.claim_seq,
       c.low  AS claim_low,
       c.mode AS claim_mode,
       c.high AS claim_high,
       c.unit AS claim_unit,
       c.origin, c.origin_absent, c.origin_derivation,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL OR e.high - e.low = 0 THEN NULL ELSE e.crossed END AS isotone,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL THEN NULL
            ELSE c.origin_derivation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'sharedParts' END AS licensed,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL      THEN 'no figure, so nothing to read'
            WHEN e.high - e.low = 0 THEN 'a point: both pairings are one arithmetic'
            WHEN e.crossed          THEN 'ISOTONE: a wider elimination widens the composed figure'
            ELSE 'ANTI-ISOTONE: a wider elimination NARROWS the composed figure'
       END AS what_a_wider_elimination_does,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL THEN 'no claim to carry an origin'
            WHEN c.origin_derivation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'sharedParts'
                 THEN 'the filer says it is a copy of the part it removes'
            WHEN c.origin_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'the filer says nothing sets this bound'
            WHEN c.origin IS NOT NULL        THEN 'the filer names who may change this bound'
            ELSE 'nobody said'
       END AS what_the_bound_rests_on
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/paired.sqlc)
) e
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = e.composition AND c.seq = e.claim_seq
